// expect-error: NITPICK-REACH-003
// expect-error-at: 62:1
//
// THE HEADER THIS FILE DID NOT HAVE UNTIL 0.0.1, AND THE REASON THAT MATTERED.
// From 2026-09-03 to 2026-09-05 this file carried no `expect-` marker at all --
// not a wrong one, none -- so it sat OUTSIDE the sweep that checks a defect
// reproduction's expectation against the compiler. It is precisely the kind of
// file that sweep exists for: on 2026-09-04 its expected behaviour changed
// completely, and no check could have noticed, because the check could not see
// it. That gap is TM-115, and the sweep now asserts its denominator.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOW: A REGRESSION CASE. O-N11 was raised from this directory,
// accepted as the compiler's DEF-5, and landed at its cycle 1.5.1b step 1b.
// The defect below NO LONGER EXISTS. `npkc` refuses this file at `main`,
// naming D-013 and LISTING the identities the absent handler would owe --
// FOUR of them, which is `SAFETY.md` S-4b's unconditional floor, because this
// file has no import, no arithmetic and no allocation:
//
//     NITPICK-REACH-003 ...: `main` without `failsafe` (D-013): ...
//       -- 4 identities: Unreachable, HeapOom, HeapBadRequest, WildLeak --
//
// A board once carried SIX for this file. Six is real and belongs to `case3`,
// which imports the raiser. The number here is four, read out of the
// compiler's own output at pin `0dfddac` and again at `94874ce` before it.
//
// AND NO `.ll` IS WRITTEN. The refusal lands one step EARLIER than it used to,
// which is the whole of what DEF-5 changed -- so the pairing this repository
// insists on (an exit code beside the artefact it should have produced) is
// exit 1 with no artefact. `TRANSCRIPT.txt` beside this file holds both
// transcripts: Part A at the current pin, Part B the original, verbatim.
//
// ==================== WHAT THE DEFECT WAS, KEPT AS WRITTEN ==================
// THE DEFECT, MINIMAL. Four lines of content: a `mod:` header and a `main`.
// There is no `failsafe`, no import, no arithmetic, no allocation.
//
// D-013 (the compiler's own `meta/specs/DECISIONS.md`): "Exactly one
// `failsafe` per program, supplied by the executable ... It is required only
// for executables and must be provided by the end user."
//
// `npkc` EXITS 0 on this file. `llc` is what refuses it, at a generated line
// number, naming an internal symbol, saying nothing about `failsafe`, nothing
// about D-013 and nothing about this file:
//
//     llc: error: /tmp/case1_no_failsafe.ll:4036:19:
//       error: use of undefined value '@npk_failsafe'
//
// STATE THE DEFECT CAREFULLY, because the obvious phrasing is wrong. "npkc
// emits calls to an undefined `@npk_failsafe`" is TRUE of this file and also
// true of every ordinary library module -- `support/probe11_arms_lib.npk`,
// which has neither `main` nor `failsafe`, emits the same seven calls, and
// that is harmless because nobody links a library module on its own. The
// transcript beside this file measures both.
//
// The defect is narrower and it is a MISSING CHECK: `npkc` does not require a
// root file that declares `main` to declare `failsafe`. It holds both facts
// already -- it emits `define i32 @main` for this file and not for a library
// module, and `reach_settle` in `src/frontend/analysis/reach.npk` tests
// `if (x.failsafe_decl == 0i32) { pass NIL; }` and returns early on exactly
// this case. It never joins them into a diagnostic.
mod:case1_no_failsafe;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    exit 0i32;
};
